1
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:06,160
It's time to pull together everything we've
learned so far to talk about workflows.

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:08,940
How do we take advantage of what the model
sees, knows,

3
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:11,316
and can do in order to make workflows that
make

4
00:00:11,316 --> 00:00:14,400
sense for us to deliver software more
effectively and faster.

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:18,160
There are a lot of different places that we
can encode the way that we work.

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:22,849
We can do it with code itself by writing
scripts, custom functionality, CI rules,

7
00:00:22,849 --> 00:00:26,960
lint rules, all those types of things to
make certain behaviors happen.

8
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:29,231
We can even write code to change how code
gets

9
00:00:29,231 --> 00:00:31,600
landed or how code is pushed up from our
agents.

10
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,970
We could rewrite the GitHub CLI or wrap it
in some

11
00:00:33,970 --> 00:00:36,720
way where we are restricting what the model
is capable of.

12
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:39,760
When models were dumber that made a lot of
sense, but they've gotten pretty smart now.

13
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:43,081
So putting your time into building custom
wrapper functions around

14
00:00:43,081 --> 00:00:45,798
your tools to prevent them from doing the
wrong thing,

15
00:00:45,798 --> 00:00:47,760
probably not the best use of your time.

16
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:51,168
The reality is that your workflows should
primarily live in places like

17
00:00:51,168 --> 00:00:54,480
your prompts themselves and the skills that
your agent has access to.

18
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:58,847
And these workflows can be very dynamic and
involve a lot of code,

19
00:00:58,847 --> 00:01:03,280
but it's using these 3 things that really
makes workflows cemented.

20
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:05,344
And where things start to get really fun is

21
00:01:05,344 --> 00:01:07,840
once you start to compose all of these
capabilities.

22
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,560
I'm going to start with a specific example
of a skill I use that I found really
helpful.

23
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:13,200
Sol Loop.

24
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,607
Sol Loop is a skill that I made because I
noticed that OpenAI's model,

25
00:01:16,607 --> 00:01:19,917
specifically GPT-5.6 Sol, might not be my
favorite for writing code,

26
00:01:19,917 --> 00:01:22,400
but they were incredibly thorough for
reviewing it.

27
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,020
So when I used models that I preferred
working with, which at the time was Fable 5,

28
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:29,247
it changes all the time, doesn't matter,

29
00:01:29,247 --> 00:01:34,201
I noticed that the GPT models were a really
good thing to bring in to give feedback on
my

30
00:01:34,201 --> 00:01:36,595
changes before I pushed them up to a PR and

31
00:01:36,595 --> 00:01:39,600
wasted my other code review agents and my
team's time.

32
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:40,880
A thing I think a lot about,

33
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,766
and I think it'll be useful to know that
this is something that's always on my brain,

34
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:45,680
is the merge button.

35
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:47,962
Merge button itself is just a button,

36
00:01:47,962 --> 00:01:51,920
but there's a lot that goes into the before
and after of that button.

37
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:55,211
Something I know about myself is I get
incredibly frustrated when

38
00:01:55,211 --> 00:01:57,034
I go to a PR and I want to merge it,

39
00:01:57,034 --> 00:01:59,920
but I can't because I don't know if the
changes are good.

40
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:03,760
I don't have a way to check the work that it
did and see if it functions or not.

41
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:06,400
Or I'm waiting on somebody else to approve
the thing.

42
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,080
Even worse though is the other side.

43
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:09,280
Once I click merge?

44
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:10,640
What if the changes were broken?

45
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:12,240
What if they do cause problems?

46
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:14,080
How do I undo the damage?

47
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:18,073
So when I think about the merge button, I
think a lot about the before,

48
00:02:18,073 --> 00:02:21,504
which is how do I make sure that by the time
I go to this PR,

49
00:02:21,504 --> 00:02:26,060
I have everything I need to verify the
changes are good and the confidence to hit

50
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:30,560
merge because I know that the code is
probably good by the time it's in that PR.

51
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:32,864
And then the other side which is if it did
end up

52
00:02:32,864 --> 00:02:35,685
bad because no matter how good you guys are
at writing code,

53
00:02:35,685 --> 00:02:39,352
even if you have the best engineers in the
world obsessively reviewing things,

54
00:02:39,352 --> 00:02:41,280
mistakes will break through your systems.

55
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:46,240
So how do you handle those mistakes and undo
the damage before it affects too many
people?

56
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:48,640
This is stuff like a staged rollout or
really

57
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:51,787
good nightly systems where beta testers
catch things early,

58
00:02:51,787 --> 00:02:54,080
a proper QA org and system, whatever it is,

59
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:58,187
making it so that merging is less scary
because by the time you're in the PR,

60
00:02:58,187 --> 00:02:59,680
it's more likely to be good.

61
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:04,518
And after you merge, if it is broken, it's
really easy to revert with a minimal amount

62
00:03:04,518 --> 00:03:09,525
of damage these are the things I think the
most about when I think about my workflows
and

63
00:03:09,525 --> 00:03:12,000
you'll see this as I describe how they work.

64
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,320
I want to be clear though you don't have to
think the way I think.

65
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:18,560
Do I think I am correct in the way I have
done the before and after of the merge
button?

66
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:19,280
Mostly yeah.

67
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:20,880
There are ups and downs for the way I think.

68
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:24,480
There's obviously systems where you can't so
simply revert a change.

69
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:27,360
Like if I was writing code for a pacemaker,
I would probably be fired.

70
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:30,583
But the way I work makes a lot more sense
for the companies I've worked at,

71
00:03:30,583 --> 00:03:31,571
like Amazon and Twitch,

72
00:03:31,571 --> 00:03:34,880
where you can stage your changes and make
sure things are good ahead of time.

73
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:36,984
But you should adjust your workflows based
on how

74
00:03:36,984 --> 00:03:38,960
you work and what isn't isn't working for
you.

75
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:42,400
But more importantly, you should be willing
to adjust them as things change.

76
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:44,720
And believe me, they are going to change a
lot.

77
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:47,840
That's why I'm choosing this Sol loop skill
as a starting point.

78
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:50,565
I know I only wrote this a few months ago
with GPT-5.6 Sol,

79
00:03:50,565 --> 00:03:53,521
but I've actually had to adjust this skill
multiple times since,

80
00:03:53,521 --> 00:03:56,800
and I think this will help you understand
the way I think about things.

81
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:00,000
So this skill was originally actually very,
very simple.

82
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,280
My first version of this skill was almost
hilariously simple.

83
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,600
Use GPT-5.6 Sol to review your work.

84
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:08,463
Keep asking for feedback and addressing it
until you

85
00:04:08,463 --> 00:04:10,720
have code that both you and Sol agree on.

86
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:15,760
You can call Sol using the Codex CLI, with
an example at the end of how to call the
CLI.

87
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,373
This immediately worked decently well and
allowed

88
00:04:18,373 --> 00:04:20,560
for Claude to get good feedback from Sol.

89
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:23,760
But I noticed some problems almost
immediately.

90
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:28,600
First off, after it did all of the Sol
loops, it would come back and say, yeah,

91
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:30,560
Sol likes the code and I do too.

92
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:32,080
But that's not where I wanted to end.

93
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:36,038
I want it to stop with a pull request that
is filed so that I have code

94
00:04:36,038 --> 00:04:40,720
that both Sol and whatever Claude model I
was using agree on so I can go merge it.

95
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:42,640
So I added that at the end here.

96
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:48,320
I added, when you both agree, file a pull
request with your changes.

97
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:49,680
And then I had a different problem.

98
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:53,217
I would go to the pull request and I saw
that Macroscope

99
00:04:53,217 --> 00:04:58,080
or Code Rabbit or Greptile caught something
else that my agents didn't catch.

100
00:04:58,080 --> 00:04:59,920
So I added another thing to the end.

101
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:05,760
Babysit the PR until all automated reviewers
and CI are green.

102
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:08,640
And then I kept the skill pretty static for
a while.

103
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:11,916
But I did notice a few little things that
were annoying,

104
00:05:11,916 --> 00:05:16,069
specifically that it would loop way longer
than it should occasionally,

105
00:05:16,069 --> 00:05:19,520
and the result of those long loops was
massive scope creep.

106
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:21,840
So I went back to the top and I adjusted
again.

107
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:27,607
Stop after 3 loops if you can't agree, push
back on nits,

108
00:05:27,607 --> 00:05:32,160
and don't let these reviews bloat your
scope.

109
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:36,160
And after all of these changes were in,
things were feeling pretty good.

110
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:41,005
But as always, I was auditing the things
that my agents were doing and asking
questions,

111
00:05:41,005 --> 00:05:45,575
and I noticed that my agents were screwing
up a lot when they were calling Codex-P,

112
00:05:45,575 --> 00:05:49,869
and a lot of the time Claude would choose to
spin up a subagent for Claude and

113
00:05:49,869 --> 00:05:52,402
then have that subagent call codex and then
it

114
00:05:52,402 --> 00:05:55,155
would read through the whole history of what
Codex

115
00:05:55,155 --> 00:05:59,229
did in the Codex CLI to get the context it
felt like it was missing and it

116
00:05:59,229 --> 00:06:02,367
ended up massively bloating the context and
increasing my

117
00:06:02,367 --> 00:06:05,120
bill for Claude by however many tokens Codex
used.

118
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:08,000
And after I noticed this, I made more
adjustments.

119
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,709
This is when I introduced the capability I
showed in the last lesson,

120
00:06:11,709 --> 00:06:16,171
which is I allowed for Sol to be used inside
of Claude on all of my devices so that

121
00:06:16,171 --> 00:06:18,644
I could just have Claude use Sol as a
subagent

122
00:06:18,644 --> 00:06:21,600
instead of having to leave the harness
entirely for it.

123
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:24,555
That said, I noticed that Claude didn't
quite understand

124
00:06:24,555 --> 00:06:27,035
how to instantiate the subagent with a
specific

125
00:06:27,035 --> 00:06:31,680
model that wasn't in its immediate family of
models that it had in some list internally.

126
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:36,080
So I ended up making a custom subagent that
was hard-coded in Claude Code that allowed
it.

127
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:38,894
So after I set up the custom Sol agent in
Claude Code,

128
00:06:38,894 --> 00:06:41,135
I changed the rule here to say you can call

129
00:06:41,135 --> 00:06:44,000
Sol using the custom Sol agent with your
subagent tool.

130
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:44,800
And that was great.

131
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:46,880
And again, I left it for a while.

132
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:50,560
But then new capabilities appeared,
specifically in T3 code.

133
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:54,894
We got the ability to have Claude call Codex
within T3 code itself,

134
00:06:54,894 --> 00:06:58,080
which was obviously better for a bunch of
reasons.

135
00:06:58,080 --> 00:06:59,200
So I changed it again.

136
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:03,920
You can call Sol using the delegate tool in
T3 code.

137
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:06,869
Initially it was actually calling this with
the new thread tool

138
00:07:06,869 --> 00:07:09,069
which annoyed me so I had to change it again
to

139
00:07:09,069 --> 00:07:11,925
specify delegate tool and I even had to make
some adjustments

140
00:07:11,925 --> 00:07:14,640
to the MCP descriptions in T3 code to get
this just right.

141
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:17,846
Again, if you rely on open source tools you
can fix these things when

142
00:07:17,846 --> 00:07:21,424
they break and you can often convince the
maintainers to take on those fixes,

143
00:07:21,424 --> 00:07:23,236
but this helps show a workflow I liked,

144
00:07:23,236 --> 00:07:27,325
which is have a different model family
review my changes and how I have adjusted it
over

145
00:07:27,325 --> 00:07:28,858
time as I used it and noticed the

146
00:07:28,858 --> 00:07:31,600
different things that weren't weren't
working how I wanted.

147
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:36,313
If you have a skill that you use regularly
that you have not edited in months,

148
00:07:36,313 --> 00:07:37,280
I have concerns.

149
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:40,839
You should spend a lot of time reading the
skills that you're using and

150
00:07:40,839 --> 00:07:45,200
modifying them to better fit the behaviors
that you do and more importantly don't want.

151
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:47,817
1 of the biggest things that was hard for me

152
00:07:47,817 --> 00:07:51,280
to accept is this idea that Markdown itself
is executable now.

153
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:52,199
Back in my day,

154
00:07:52,199 --> 00:07:57,286
the idea that you could have something.js
and pipe this into Node.js and have it do

155
00:07:57,286 --> 00:08:00,718
something was just mind-blowing to me
because JavaScript

156
00:08:00,718 --> 00:08:03,600
was this weird language you ran in the
browser.

157
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:07,200
It wasn't meant to be an executable thing
you ran in your CLIs.

158
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:10,996
But now you can take something.markdown and
pipe that into

159
00:08:10,996 --> 00:08:14,400
Claude and it will execute it almost like
it's code.

160
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:15,760
Is it deterministic?

161
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:19,600
No, but I'll be real, JavaScript barely
feels deterministic nowadays.

162
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:23,874
So yeah, getting this into my head changed
how I build entirely and

163
00:08:23,874 --> 00:08:29,169
I realized that a lot of the processes that
I had, a lot of the code I had written,

164
00:08:29,169 --> 00:08:34,400
a lot of the side projects I had built could
just actually be a markdown file now.

165
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:39,120
And for some things that are dynamic and
strange, this actually is quite nice.

166
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:41,440
And there's a lot of different ways that you
could do this.

167
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:45,827
As you can probably guess, with a big open
source project like T3 Code,

168
00:08:45,827 --> 00:08:47,680
we get a ton of pull requests.

169
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:51,280
We get as many as 800 to 1,000 a day
sometimes.

170
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:54,485
And getting through all of this is nearly
impossible and we're always going to

171
00:08:54,485 --> 00:08:57,280
miss some easy wins that people have put up
as pull requests for us.

172
00:08:57,280 --> 00:08:57,920
And I hate that.

173
00:08:57,920 --> 00:08:58,800
It makes me feel bad.

174
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:04,640
So 1 of the things that I built is a
scheduled prompt that runs every day at 9
p.m.

175
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:08,924
that will go through the T3 code codebase
and ask it to triage

176
00:09:08,924 --> 00:09:13,229
open PRs that were opened or updated in the
last 24 hours and then

177
00:09:13,229 --> 00:09:17,926
come back with the results and tell me
specifically what are some simple

178
00:09:17,926 --> 00:09:21,840
PRs that I should go take a look at that are
ready to merge.

179
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:23,723
Since we get so many pull requests,

180
00:09:23,723 --> 00:09:28,080
doing all of this with an expensive model
like Opus would put us out of business.

181
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:31,760
So I have it using Haiku to do the
individual PR audits.

182
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:35,363
And then when Haiku flags some that it
thinks are really good,

183
00:09:35,363 --> 00:09:37,920
it then passes that up to Opus to finish it.

184
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,360
The craziest thing here though is that no
code was written.

185
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:44,000
I didn't program anything for this flow.

186
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,160
Here is the super complex workflow I built
here.

187
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:48,400
Every day at 9 p.m., I want you to do the
following.

188
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:50,642
Spin up a set of haiku 5.5 delegates to look
at

189
00:09:50,642 --> 00:09:53,409
pull requests that have had activity in the
last 24 hours,

190
00:09:53,409 --> 00:09:56,414
do a brief audit of these PRs for
mergeability, value, novelty,

191
00:09:56,414 --> 00:09:58,560
and other insights that I might want to
know.

192
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:02,294
If there's any that look mergeable, have
Opus 5.5 and 61 Sol delegates,

193
00:10:02,294 --> 00:10:06,186
take a look and verify, and then come back
to me with a short list of 8 or

194
00:10:06,186 --> 00:10:09,920
less PRs that you think are worth merging or
taking over to get landed.

195
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:11,920
Any confusion about what I'm looking for
here?

196
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:13,040
Ask if so.

197
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:16,480
Otherwise, do a test run before we set up
the scheduled task.

198
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:18,880
And you can bet your butt that's exactly
what it did.

199
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:21,680
It started to test with those haiku
delegates.

200
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:24,080
It came back with all of the things that
they found.

201
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:28,000
They had these 2 or they had Opus 5.5 and 61
Sol review these 12 PRs.

202
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:29,444
And finally at the end here,

203
00:10:29,444 --> 00:10:32,640
I got my list of things that it thought I
should go and merge.

204
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:35,200
I said, this looks good, set it with a
schedule.

205
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:38,480
And it wrote this prompt that it reruns
every night for me.

206
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:41,017
It even has things that kind of feel like
code,

207
00:10:41,017 --> 00:10:44,093
like specific commands it should run and
things it can do

208
00:10:44,093 --> 00:10:47,655
to avoid hitting my limits and rate limits
specifically on GitHub,

209
00:10:47,655 --> 00:10:49,760
which is a thing I'm fighting actively.

210
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:52,086
I find myself hitting the GitHub rate limits
even more

211
00:10:52,086 --> 00:10:55,360
than my agent limits at this point to each
their own, but I hit those a lot.

212
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:57,620
The main point I'm trying to make here is

213
00:10:57,620 --> 00:11:00,320
that markdown can be used as the workflow
itself.

214
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:01,680
I wrote a prompt.

215
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:05,920
The model adjusted the prompt after noticing
certain things that were and weren't
working.

216
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:09,360
It created this flow that auto-executes at 9
p.m.

217
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:12,160
and makes my life easier and helps me land
more code.

218
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:14,094
There's another underlying piece here that I

219
00:11:14,094 --> 00:11:16,160
really want to make sure you understand
though.

220
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:19,840
The idea that we shouldn't be avoiding
tedious stuff, we should be seeking it.

221
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:23,288
Historically as engineers we have always
tried to avoid the tedious stuff,

222
00:11:23,288 --> 00:11:25,431
but if the tedious thing was important
enough,

223
00:11:25,431 --> 00:11:27,760
we would write a bunch of code to make it go
away.

224
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:30,753
Great engineers hate tedium, and that's what
makes them great engineers,

225
00:11:30,753 --> 00:11:32,748
because when they hate something that's
tedious,

226
00:11:32,748 --> 00:11:36,240
they'll either avoid it or they will write a
solution to never think about it again.

227
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:41,005
We gotta get over that now because tedious
stuff that we've historically avoided,

228
00:11:41,005 --> 00:11:46,240
like reviewing every single PR by hand, that
tedious stuff is what agents were built for.

229
00:11:46,240 --> 00:11:47,200
They love it.

230
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:51,440
If the task is so boring that you feel your
eyes glazing over as you do it.

231
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:53,857
That is incredible work for your agents to
do and you

232
00:11:53,857 --> 00:11:56,320
should be doing 10 times more of it using
your agents.

233
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:59,993
I could never even come close to reviewing
all the PRs that we are getting,

234
00:11:59,993 --> 00:12:03,274
but it doesn't matter because I've built
systems and workflows with

235
00:12:03,274 --> 00:12:06,017
my agents to bring the important things up
so that I can

236
00:12:06,017 --> 00:12:08,906
focus my attention on the things that aren't
tedious and in

237
00:12:08,906 --> 00:12:11,600
my case are more fraught fun but also a bit
more scary.

238
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:15,360
And you can build workflows for all sorts of
different things that you do every day.

239
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:19,760
For example, I have very specific ways I
like pull requests to be written.

240
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:21,807
I want them to clearly state what the
problem

241
00:12:21,807 --> 00:12:24,400
is and then simply describe the solution
that was chosen.

242
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:29,680
And I found that agents get very verbose and
gross when they file pull requests.

243
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:33,040
So I made my own file PR skill for filing
concise PRs.

244
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:35,760
Use whenever the user asks to file, open, or
create a PR.

245
00:12:35,760 --> 00:12:39,074
Also note that my skills almost never have
the restriction

246
00:12:39,074 --> 00:12:41,760
and that they can only be invoked by the
human.

247
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:43,440
I don't want user invoked skills.

248
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:45,520
There's a bunch of weird implementation
details there.

249
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:46,320
I don't care.

250
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:48,551
I want my skills to be clear when the agent
should

251
00:12:48,551 --> 00:12:50,960
or shouldn't use them and I let the agent
choose when.

252
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:52,779
In this case I want it to use the

253
00:12:52,779 --> 00:12:55,920
skill whenever I am filing or dealing with
pull requests.

254
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:59,543
So it starts very simply with making sure
that there isn't already a PR for this,

255
00:12:59,543 --> 00:13:01,600
because it has made mistakes with that
before.

256
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:04,800
I then say PR titles are often commit
messages.

257
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:06,720
So follow the repos title conventions.

258
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:09,360
Look at recently merged PRs and git history
for examples.

259
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:12,640
Prefer a concise human readable title that
explains why the change matters.

260
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:17,520
So a bad 1 would be perf server, negotiate
per message deflate on the WebSocket.

261
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:19,520
I have no idea what the fuck this means when
I read it.

262
00:13:19,520 --> 00:13:21,600
But a good example would be perf server.

263
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:25,280
Cut WebSocket frame size by 70% plus with
G-zipping.

264
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:28,800
Believe it or not, these are real examples
that I had.

265
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:32,590
It actually wrote this commit message and it
pissed me off so much that I

266
00:13:32,590 --> 00:13:36,640
opened up VS Code and I wrote a skill to
prevent it from ever happening again.

267
00:13:36,640 --> 00:13:40,300
And after spending 2 minutes writing this
markdown file,

268
00:13:40,300 --> 00:13:44,418
I have had all of the branch names, PR
titles, commit messages,

269
00:13:44,418 --> 00:13:46,640
and all of that be so much better.

270
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:49,680
And agents are so good with examples like
this too.

271
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:52,270
If you have a thing you don't like that your
agent did,

272
00:13:52,270 --> 00:13:54,860
you can encode that with a skill and steer
the model to

273
00:13:54,860 --> 00:13:57,215
go there when it's a thing that it is about
to do,

274
00:13:57,215 --> 00:14:00,323
and it will see the bad example, which is
what it would have done,

275
00:14:00,323 --> 00:14:03,760
and it'll see the good example, which is
what you want, and it will work.

276
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:05,600
It works unreasonably well.

277
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:08,560
I also have a similar thing for the PR
description.

278
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:11,920
The bad example was removed implicit
workspace carryover.

279
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:15,760
I don't know what the hell it means by this,
so I wrote a better example.

280
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:20,080
My new work tree default was ignored when
starting new threads on existing work trees.

281
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:21,360
Super unintuitive.

282
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:24,320
Now my or now your preferences should always
apply.

283
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:25,280
There you go.

284
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:27,253
As you start to build your workflows,

285
00:14:27,253 --> 00:14:30,720
1 of the important things I push is this
idea of prompting wider.

286
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:32,800
There's 2 different directions you can go
with your prompts.

287
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:36,240
You can have them go deeper where the model
will go super analyze something.

288
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:39,440
Maybe it's like rewriting a function in Rust
or something like that.

289
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:40,480
That is depth.

290
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:43,520
The model is going deeper and solving harder
and harder problems.

291
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:45,600
There are limits to how deep the agents can
go.

292
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:47,494
They're much farther than I ever would have
expected,

293
00:14:47,494 --> 00:14:48,960
but that's not what I want to talk about.

294
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:51,200
There's plenty of other lessons that you're
going to hear for that.

295
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:53,971
I want to talk about the idea of prompting
wider,

296
00:14:53,971 --> 00:14:57,760
expanding the start points and end points of
where the prompt goes.

297
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:00,051
Back in the day we used to just autocomplete
and

298
00:15:00,051 --> 00:15:02,533
then we moved to Command K and making fixed
changes,

299
00:15:02,533 --> 00:15:06,160
then we moved to the agent and telling it
which files to go make changes in.

300
00:15:06,160 --> 00:15:08,674
I remember how crazy it was when cursor
added the change

301
00:15:08,674 --> 00:15:10,873
where the agent had a list of the files that
were

302
00:15:10,873 --> 00:15:13,297
open in your currently open tabs so that it
knew which

303
00:15:13,297 --> 00:15:16,080
files were there without having to do
something custom for it.

304
00:15:16,080 --> 00:15:19,612
All of these are expanding the width of how
we use these things because

305
00:15:19,612 --> 00:15:23,144
you have to do less before you bring in the
agent and more importantly,

306
00:15:23,144 --> 00:15:25,680
the agent can go further before it needs you
again.

307
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:28,600
If you are still telling the agent which
file to edit

308
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:32,016
and then verifying each changed line before
getting into a PR,

309
00:15:32,016 --> 00:15:34,000
you need this part more than anyone.

310
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,036
And you should probably send this to your
team

311
00:15:36,036 --> 00:15:38,160
members as well if they are stuck in that
space.

312
00:15:38,160 --> 00:15:42,178
Because I now can bring the agent in as soon
as I know a problem exists,

313
00:15:42,178 --> 00:15:44,689
I can literally send my agent a screenshot
of

314
00:15:44,689 --> 00:15:47,424
a DM I got from somebody complaining about a
bug,

315
00:15:47,424 --> 00:15:51,944
and by the time I check the thread again,
there's a pull request up, ready to go,

316
00:15:51,944 --> 00:15:54,400
with a video showing exactly what was wrong.

317
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:57,440
And a video confirming the fix actually
works.

318
00:15:57,440 --> 00:15:59,200
That is width of the prompts.

319
00:15:59,200 --> 00:16:04,240
I'm bringing the agent in earlier and it's
able to go longer before it needs me again.

320
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:07,440
And a lot of the workflows I'm building are
in order to enable that.

321
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:09,803
I don't want the agent to bother me until
it's

322
00:16:09,803 --> 00:16:12,577
done everything it can to verify the changes
are good,

323
00:16:12,577 --> 00:16:16,533
then it can bring me in and ask me to take
it 1 last look, give it thumbs up,

324
00:16:16,533 --> 00:16:17,920
and then finally hit merge.

325
00:16:17,920 --> 00:16:20,480
Sometimes I go even further though and
that's what this skill is for.

326
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:22,320
This 1's called full sent.

327
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:24,000
I have the description, it's very simple.

328
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,480
Only trigger if the user requests full send.

329
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:30,001
If the user triggered this, it's because
they are not watching the thread and they

330
00:16:30,001 --> 00:16:32,320
want you to complete the work end to end
autonomously.

331
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:36,400
Your updates and your final message will go
unread long after you stop.

332
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:39,040
You own the work from its current state to a
merged PR.

333
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:40,320
And I have steps it should follow.

334
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:43,029
First it should make the change, then it
should prove the change,

335
00:16:43,029 --> 00:16:45,280
prove that it actually was able to get the
thing done.

336
00:16:45,280 --> 00:16:47,520
Then it should get feedback using the Sol
loop skill.

337
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:48,960
It's another important piece here.

338
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:51,040
My skills are all composed.

339
00:16:51,040 --> 00:16:53,840
They can stack on each other where it makes
sense to.

340
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:56,960
You can tell your agents in 1 skill to call
another 1.

341
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:58,160
It's all a graph.

342
00:16:58,160 --> 00:17:01,680
The agents are very good at calling things
from other things.

343
00:17:01,680 --> 00:17:05,128
And once you realize that Markdown is kind
of just a program at this point,

344
00:17:05,128 --> 00:17:07,427
you can have your program call another
program the

345
00:17:07,427 --> 00:17:09,680
same way we would back in the Unix terminal
days.

346
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:14,517
And once you build the right chunks, which
for me are the Sol loop skill,

347
00:17:14,517 --> 00:17:18,692
my file PR skill, my babysit PR skill, my
comment format skill,

348
00:17:18,692 --> 00:17:22,800
my debug and process changes skills, my
verify changes skills.

349
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:25,920
Some of these are repo specific, a lot of
them are general for me.

350
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:27,997
These skills are the building blocks that

351
00:17:27,997 --> 00:17:30,480
these higher level workflow skills can hook
into.

352
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:35,676
And you'll see here this 1 hooks into Sol
loop, file PR, babysit, watch pull request,

353
00:17:35,676 --> 00:17:36,960
my file upload skill.

354
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:40,082
This 1's really important because GitHub, up
until recently,

355
00:17:40,082 --> 00:17:43,360
wouldn't let you upload files via the CLI,
which was obnoxious.

356
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:47,400
I've seen so many times where somebody's
changes added a giant video file

357
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:51,440
to the repo because the agent wanted to
include it in the PR description.

358
00:17:51,440 --> 00:17:54,227
So I have a custom file upload skill and
tool I built in order

359
00:17:54,227 --> 00:17:57,418
to make that workflow easier because I want
to see the video in the PR,

360
00:17:57,418 --> 00:18:00,160
but I don't want it committing the frickin'
video to my repo.

361
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:02,412
And obviously not everyone can use a skill
like

362
00:18:02,412 --> 00:18:05,287
full send where the model's autonomously
merging the changes

363
00:18:05,287 --> 00:18:08,977
so that I never see it because uh 1 of the
features in T3 code is that once a

364
00:18:08,977 --> 00:18:11,852
change is merged the thread will
auto-archive itself because

365
00:18:11,852 --> 00:18:13,960
why do you need it the change merged so when

366
00:18:13,960 --> 00:18:16,164
I use full send I'm mentally prepared to
never

367
00:18:16,164 --> 00:18:18,560
see the threat again unless it asks me a
question.

368
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:19,920
But that doesn't always work.

369
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:21,760
And I was addicted to this skill.

370
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:23,600
It became my favorite thing to use.

371
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:26,880
So I ended up making an adjacent version
called full stop.

372
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:28,400
Full stop is very similar.

373
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:33,040
The difference is it does everything up
until the merge button can be pressed.

374
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:36,960
And then it tells me, hey ***, time to come
in and take a look at this.

375
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:41,200
Everything else is effectively the same,
pretty much the same flow.

376
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:43,706
I just wanted it to go as far as possible
and

377
00:18:43,706 --> 00:18:46,880
not bother me until it thinks the code is
ready to merge.

378
00:18:46,880 --> 00:18:51,749
So I've created this hierarchy here
effectively where I have the skills the repo
provides,

379
00:18:51,749 --> 00:18:54,887
the skills that I provide, like the building
block skills,

380
00:18:54,887 --> 00:18:57,105
then I have a workflow set on top where I

381
00:18:57,105 --> 00:19:01,920
have the specific way I want it to resolve
an issue, whether it's full send or full
stop.

382
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:03,763
Then above that I have the prompt I send,

383
00:19:03,763 --> 00:19:07,360
which is what details the problem and which
of those paths I want it to go down.

384
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:10,273
So when I see a bug that's a really simple
small thing,

385
00:19:10,273 --> 00:19:14,881
like the CSS isn't aligned properly, I take
a screenshot of it, I hand it to the agent,

386
00:19:14,881 --> 00:19:18,800
I say fix it, full send, and then the bug
will be fixed by the next build.

387
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:23,565
If I notice something a bit hairier, like
the logic for how data is getting to the
harness

388
00:19:23,565 --> 00:19:27,165
from my UI or that the database isn't
persisting something properly,

389
00:19:27,165 --> 00:19:31,930
there's like a memory leak, then I will send
as much info as I know with full stop
saying,

390
00:19:31,930 --> 00:19:34,419
go explore this to the best of your ability
and

391
00:19:34,419 --> 00:19:37,120
come back to me when you think you have a
solution.

392
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:39,693
And sometimes I just want to go back and
forth with the

393
00:19:39,693 --> 00:19:42,640
model to figure out what I want and how we
should implement it.

394
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:46,000
That's when things like Matt Pocock's
grilling skill come into play.

395
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:49,760
I'll be real with y'all, I think I have 2
times more skills than I probably should
here.

396
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:52,720
I was playing with a lot of the P stack and
Matt skills.

397
00:19:52,720 --> 00:19:53,600
They're really good.

398
00:19:53,600 --> 00:19:55,041
You should definitely explore them,

399
00:19:55,041 --> 00:19:57,800
but you should also not be that scared to
delete skills when you're

400
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:00,560
not using them that much or if they're doing
things you don't want.

401
00:20:00,560 --> 00:20:03,985
And of course, you can ask your agent which
skills are being invoked where,

402
00:20:03,985 --> 00:20:06,086
find the history of how often these skills
are

403
00:20:06,086 --> 00:20:10,151
being pulled in and how often I end up
merging the change after the skill is pulled
in to

404
00:20:10,151 --> 00:20:12,800
figure out which ones are and aren't
actually helping you.

405
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:16,748
So how do you decide which skills should be
global, which skills should be 1 harness,

406
00:20:16,748 --> 00:20:18,560
and which skills should go in the repo?

407
00:20:18,560 --> 00:20:22,103
Generally speaking, if the skill is useful
in more than 1 repo,

408
00:20:22,103 --> 00:20:24,240
it should probably be in your globals.

409
00:20:24,240 --> 00:20:28,517
If the point of the skill is to keep you and
your coworkers from making dumb mistakes,

410
00:20:28,517 --> 00:20:29,760
then it goes in the repo.

411
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:33,120
1 of the skills I have found the most value
in is a really simple building block.

412
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:34,880
It's my PR comment skill.

413
00:20:34,880 --> 00:20:37,225
The point of the skill is mostly to make
sure

414
00:20:37,225 --> 00:20:39,883
my agents call out that they are an agent
leaving a

415
00:20:39,883 --> 00:20:42,749
comment on my behalf instead of it just
leaving a bunch

416
00:20:42,749 --> 00:20:46,240
of comments and not clarifying that it was
Claude or Codex, not me.

417
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:50,640
So I made this to make it very clear when an
agent is responding on my behalf.

418
00:20:50,640 --> 00:20:51,520
This was great.

419
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:56,169
It was so great that I would get really
annoyed when I saw other people contributing

420
00:20:56,169 --> 00:21:00,320
comments and PRs and not indicating if it
was a human or a bot that did it.

421
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:02,800
So I took the skill and I put it in the
repo.

422
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:06,086
And now every time somebody's agent leaves a
comment in the repo,

423
00:21:06,086 --> 00:21:10,080
I know what model they used, what harness
they used, and if it was them or not.

424
00:21:10,080 --> 00:21:15,008
And if the comment doesn't have a call-out
like this, either it was a human who left
it,

425
00:21:15,008 --> 00:21:19,488
or they are using a really bad harness that
couldn't find the skill in the repo,

426
00:21:19,488 --> 00:21:21,840
either way I can now treat it differently.

427
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:25,180
So a really simple way of thinking about
this is that the point of

428
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:29,380
a repo specific skill is to prevent bad
behavior that other contributors could hit,

429
00:21:29,380 --> 00:21:33,176
whether it is a thing the model's confused
by that you can't fix with code,

430
00:21:33,176 --> 00:21:35,251
or it is a specific thing that people are

431
00:21:35,251 --> 00:21:37,984
doing that is making your life as a
maintainer harder,

432
00:21:37,984 --> 00:21:40,160
or even just as a coworker or collaborator.

433
00:21:40,160 --> 00:21:44,617
Workflow skills can be useful for complex
enough repos, but generally speaking,

434
00:21:44,617 --> 00:21:47,325
I try to keep my workflow skills slightly
higher

435
00:21:47,325 --> 00:21:49,920
level to be useful outside of 1 specific
repo.

436
00:21:49,920 --> 00:21:52,979
And you can take these base skills, these
building blocks,

437
00:21:52,979 --> 00:21:55,669
and combine them to workflow style skills
that lets

438
00:21:55,669 --> 00:21:58,570
you take advantage of all these pieces
you've built and

439
00:21:58,570 --> 00:22:01,155
encode flows that will make you more
effective at

440
00:22:01,155 --> 00:22:04,320
delivering software the way you and your
team actually want.

441
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:07,440
Did you notice how little we talked about
the code itself in this?

442
00:22:07,440 --> 00:22:08,240
There's a reason.

443
00:22:08,480 --> 00:22:10,320
The code just isn't flexible enough.

444
00:22:10,320 --> 00:22:12,380
If you build out all these workflows to be

445
00:22:12,380 --> 00:22:15,519
really specific ways that things are
executed in your harnesses,

446
00:22:15,519 --> 00:22:17,873
then you're not taking advantage of how
powerful

447
00:22:17,873 --> 00:22:20,080
in general these models and harnesses can
